the Duke and Duchess of Cambridge know  these tours can always be a diplomatic  dance after years of difficult brexit  negotiations between the UK and Ireland  William wasn't shying away from the fact  that damage has been done and  relationships need to be rebuilt  of course the changing relationship  between the UK and the EU will require  us to work together to ensure that the  relationship between Ireland and the UK  remains just as strong legal treaties  are vital in underpinning the  relationships between states but  relationships between people are equally  if not more essential especially between  the people of our two countries whose  lives histories and futures are so  deeply intertwined adding that his  generation must never forget the efforts  that were made for peace it is right  that we continue to remember those who  suffered as a consequence of our  troubled past and whilst many wrongs  have been done it is important that we  are not bound by these it is vital the  people of my generation and generations  to come never take for granted the  progress we have made together  we must recommit ourselves to the path  of friendship and understanding a  message of friendship viewed is highly  significant by their Irish hosts  [Music]  since the referendum we've heard many  speeches from members of the royal  family on European tours but this is  probably the most significant yet with  Prince William actually talking about  the UK leaving the EU and now the  importance of rebuilding relationships  and friendships that have been damaged  by breakfast it's not their place to be  political but walk along the cliffs to  look out over the Irish Sea towards the  UK was more than just a gentle photo  opportunity a reminder that we are  neighbors with a shared history and  shared bond that the royal family are  determined to play their part to protect  Rhiannon Mills Sky News in Dublin
 

